.TH BUTTERVIEW 1 "October 2026" "butterview 0.4.5" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
butterview \- a small, fast image viewer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B butterview
.RI [ file-or-folder ]
.br
.B butterview
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B butterview
shows one image at a time with a strip of the folder's thumbnails across
the top and a status line underneath. Given a file it opens that image
with its folder in the strip; given a folder it opens the first image in
it; given nothing it uses the current directory.
.PP
It is a viewer first. Rotating and cropping change what is on screen and
nothing else; saving writes a new file beside the original and never
overwrites it.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Left, Right, Space
Previous and next image. Past the last image, Right moves on to the next
sibling folder that holds images, from its first; before the first, Left
goes to the previous one, from its last.
.TP
.B Home, End, PgUp, PgDn
First and last image; ten at a time.
.TP
.B wheel, +, \-
Zoom, centered on the pointer.
.TP
.B drag
Pan a zoomed image.
.TP
.B 0, 1, double\-click
Fit to the window, actual size, and switch between the two.
.TP
.B f, F11
Full screen: the picture alone, without the strip or the status line.
Esc leaves it;
.B t
shows the strip anyway. The window manager does the resizing; under one
that only pretends (dwm with the fakefullscreen patch) use the window
manager's own fullscreen key instead.
.TP
.B t
Thumbnail strip on and off. Click a thumbnail to jump to it, scroll over
the strip to move through it.
.TP
.B i
Image information: size, dates and, for photographs, the camera, lens,
shutter speed, aperture, ISO, focal length and location from EXIF.
.TP
.B r, Shift\-r
Rotate right and left, on screen.
.TP
.B x
Crop. A rectangle appears; drag to choose the area, Enter saves a copy,
Esc cancels.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-s
Save what is on screen (rotated, cropped) as a copy.
.TP
.B right\-click
All of the above as a menu.
.TP
.B ?, F1
The keys, on screen. The status line points to both until one has
been opened.
.TP
.B Backspace, Enter
The folder view: the folders as a tree down the left, from the home
folder and from
.IR / ,
with the path to the folder shown already open and the number of images
in each folder beside it (counting stops at 500); the folder's thumbnails
in a grid on the right. In the tree Up and Down move, Right opens a
branch or steps into it, Left closes one or steps out, Enter shows the
folder. Tab moves to the grid, where the arrows move and Enter opens the
image. A folder's arrow, its name and a thumbnail do the same with the
mouse; + and \- size the thumbnails. A line over the grid names the
folder, the count and the sort order. Backspace or Esc closes the view.
.TP
.B s
Sort order: by name (naturally, so img2 comes before img10), newest
first, oldest first. Kept between runs, with the thumbnail size and the
hidden switch, in
.IR $XDG_STATE_HOME/butterview/state .
.TP
.B ., Ctrl\-h
Hidden files and folders (dot-names) shown or left out. Left out, the
tree still shows the hidden folders on the way to the folder shown.
.TP
.B o
Open a folder with the desktop's own dialog, asked for through the XDG
desktop portal's FileChooser interface. Where no portal backend answers
the status line says so. A folder or an image dropped on the window from
a file manager is opened the same way.
.TP
.B y, Ctrl\-c
Copy the image to the clipboard.
.TP
.B w
Set the image as the wallpaper with the command on the
.B wallpaper =
line of
.IR ~/.config/butterview/butterview.conf ,
the image's path added on the end, as in
.BR "wallpaper = awww img" .
A line that has
.B """$1"""
in it is run as written, with the path there:
.BR "wallpaper = pkill swaybg; swaybg \-i ""$1"" &" .
Lines starting with
.B #
are skipped. Examples for other setters are in
.IR /usr/share/doc/butterview/butterview.conf.example .
.TP
.B Del
Move the image to the trash, after a confirmation dialog. Enter, y or the
button confirm; anything else keeps the file.
.TP
.B q, Esc
Quit. Esc first backs out of the menu, crop and full screen.
.SH SAVED COPIES
Saving writes a new file beside the original, named
.IR photo\-rotated.jpg ,
.I photo\-cropped.jpg
or
.I photo\-edited.jpg
when both, with
.IR \-2 ,
.I \-3
appended if the name is taken. PNGs stay PNG and lossless; JPEGs are
re\-encoded at quality 95 without their EXIF block. Other formats are
saved as PNG. The viewer then shows the new file.
.SH FORMATS
JPEG, PNG, GIF, WebP, AVIF, JPEG XL, TIFF, SVG, BMP, QOI and TGA.
Animated GIFs play; an animated WebP shows its first frame. JPEGs are shown the way the camera says (EXIF orientation).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the keys and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
For checking a change without a screen:
.TP
.BI \-\-shot " out.png"
Render one frame to a PNG and exit, with no window.
.TP
.BI \-\-size " WxH"
Frame size for
.BR \-\-shot " (default 1280x800)."
.TP
.BI \-\-goto " N"
Start on the Nth image of the folder.
.TP
.BI \-\-zoom " factor"
Start at that zoom.
.TP
.BI \-\-keys " list"
Comma\-separated keys to press before the shot, as in
.BR "\-\-keys r,save" .
.SH COLORS
Colors come from the first of these that defines any:
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/butterview/colors.conf
(lines of
.IR "key = #rrggbb" ,
keys
.BR background ", " surface ", " text ", " muted ", " accent ", " border ),
the GTK4 theme in
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/gtk\-4.0/gtk.css
(following one
.BR @import ,
which is how KDE and COSMIC pass GTK apps their colors),
the GTK3 theme named in
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/gtk\-3.0/settings.ini
(or in xfconf, on Xfce;
.B gtk\-dark.css
when dark is preferred), else the desktop's answer over the portal:
dark or light, and its accent color, painted onto a built\-in set.
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME
defaults to
.IR ~/.config .
They are re\-read when the window gains focus.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BUTTERVIEW_TIMING
When set, print each start\-up phase, where the colors came from, the
status\-line messages and the moment the image is on screen to the
terminal.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the files below live; see COLORS.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/butterview/colors.conf
Color overrides, see COLORS.
.TP
.I ~/.config/butterview/butterview.conf
The
.B wallpaper =
command for the
.B w
key.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/butterview/butterview.conf.example
That file with a line for each common wallpaper setter, commented out.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gio (1),
.BR wl\-copy (1),
.BR xclip (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Drew (justaguylinux). Reports and patches:
.UR https://justaguy.dev/drew/butterview
.UE
